# Build-context hygiene for the web image.
#
# `docker-compose.yml` builds this app with `context: apps/web`, so this file — not
# the repo-root `.dockerignore` — is what governs it.
#
# This file exists because of what was measured without it: `load build context`
# transferred 1.18 GB and took 214.3 s on its own, because the build stage's
# `COPY . .` was being handed the host `node_modules` (the bulk of it — `du -sm` on
# it does not finish inside 60 s on this machine, which is why the BuildKit transfer
# total is the number quoted), the previous `.next` output at 293 MB, and
# `db/custom.db` — a 929,792-byte SQLite file holding the developer's last demo run.
# Every one of those is re-created inside the image: `deps` installs from
# package.json + bun.lock, `build` runs `next build`, and the runner writes its own
# database under `/app/db` on a named volume.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored: `tests/` and `e2e/`. `tsconfig.json` includes
# `**/*.ts`/`**/*.tsx` and `next.config.ts` sets `typescript.ignoreBuildErrors:
# false`, so a container build reads the suite's type errors. Excluding them would
# produce a faster image that silently stops being a check on the code it ships.

# --- re-created by the build itself ---
node_modules
.next
out
build

# --- runtime state: /app/db is a volume, seeded by the `schema` stage ---
db
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal

# --- host-only config and secrets ---
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* and DATABASE_URL come from docker-compose.yml / the environment;
# a `.env` file baked into a published image is how a development override ends up
# in production (the compose file defaults MEDISAATHI_ALLOW_DEMO_RESET to 0 — the
# local .env.development sets it to 1, and that belongs on this machine only).
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- evidence from previous local runs ---
test-results
playwright-report
coverage
screenshots

# --- build noise ---
*.log
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
.turbo
.eslintcache
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Windows symlink stand-ins ---
# `node_modules/.bin` entries are `.cmd` shims on this host; on the Linux side they
# are real symlinks. Either way the deps stage regenerates them.
**/*.cmd
